======================================================================
6. DEFINITIVE PRIMARY ENDPOINTS
======================================================================

The core mechanical endpoints are:

6.1 Peak myocardial von Mises stress

Definition:
Maximum von Mises stress within the modeled ventricular myocardium
according to the final analysis definition.

Unit:
kPa

Role:
Primary tissue-level mechanical loading endpoint.


6.2 Peak principal strain

Definition:
Maximum principal myocardial strain according to the predefined
post-processing convention.

Unit:
%

Role:
Primary tissue-deformation endpoint.


6.3 Maximum ventricular displacement

Definition:
Maximum displacement magnitude within the modeled ventricular
structure.

Unit:
mm

Role:
Primary structural-deformation endpoint.


6.4 LV cavity volume

Definition:
Computed left-ventricular cavity volume according to the geometry/
post-processing definition used in the computational workflow.

Unit:
mL

Role:
Primary chamber-level structural endpoint.


6.5 Myocardial volume

Definition:
Volume of the modeled ventricular myocardial tissue.

Unit:
mL

Role:
Structural/remodeling-related geometric endpoint where applicable.

Important:
Myocardial-volume changes caused by the computational deformation
state must not automatically be described as biological remodeling.


6.6 Total ventricular strain energy

Definition:
Integrated strain energy of the modeled ventricular myocardium.

Unit:
J

Role:
Primary energetic/mechanical endpoint.


======================================================================
7. SECONDARY / DERIVED GRAVITY-RESPONSE METRICS
======================================================================

Where appropriate and supported by the underlying numerical outputs,
gravity-response quantities may be expressed relative to the 1.00 g
reference condition.

For endpoint X:

Normalized response:

X_normalized(gamma)
=
X(gamma) / X(1.00 g) × 100

Relative change from 1.00 g:

Delta_X(%)
=
[X(gamma) - X(1.00 g)] / X(1.00 g) × 100

The 1.00 g condition is therefore the within-model reference condition
for gravity-response normalization.

Relative changes must be calculated from the underlying patient-level
values and not manually adjusted to reproduce a target percentage.


