======================================================================
21. PROHIBITED RESULT CONVERSIONS
======================================================================

The following practices are prohibited:

- inventing patient-level results to fill missing cells;
- modifying values to match a figure;
- modifying values to match a previously drafted table;
- replacing unexpected numerical results with "expected" values;
- back-calculating individual patient results from a group mean;
- changing SD values to make them appear more plausible;
- changing R² or error metrics to improve apparent performance;
- treating planned simulations as executed;
- treating a model assumption as an observed physiological finding.


======================================================================
22. VALIDATION DISTINCTION
======================================================================

This gravity experiment is separate from calibration and external
validation.

The gravity conditions are experimental model inputs.

They are not validation targets.

If an independent validation dataset is subsequently established, it
must be documented separately through the calibration/model-lock and
independent-validation registries.

External physiological observations must not be used to alter the
gravity-response results after the primary model has been locked.


======================================================================
23. LIMITATION CONTROL

The present experiment is a finite-element ventricular
solid-mechanics study.

Therefore, unless independently supported by executable model evidence,
the gravity experiment should not be represented as demonstrating:

- complete cardiovascular adaptation;
- systemic circulatory adaptation;
- autonomic adaptation;
- baroreflex resetting;
- chronic biological remodeling;
- astronaut-specific physiological prediction;
- blood-flow dynamics;
- wall shear stress;
- full fluid-structure interaction;
- electrophysiological adaptation.

The present computational experiment establishes gravity-dependent
mechanical behavior within the modeled ventricular solid-mechanics
framework.


======================================================================
24. MANUSCRIPT-READY EXPERIMENTAL DESCRIPTION
======================================================================

The gravity-response experiment was designed using patient-specific
finite-element ventricular models derived from publicly available
cardiac imaging data. Each computational subject was evaluated under six
predefined gravitational loading conditions (1.00, 0.75, 0.50, 0.38,
0.16, and 0.00 g). The baseline patient-specific geometry, mesh,
constitutive formulation, fiber-orientation mapping, material parameters,
and numerical configuration were held fixed across the primary gravity
sweep unless explicitly specified as gravity-dependent model inputs.
Primary mechanical outputs included peak myocardial von Mises stress,
peak principal strain, maximum ventricular displacement, LV cavity
volume, myocardial volume, and total ventricular strain energy.
Patient-level outputs were retained for each executed simulation and
were subsequently summarized across subjects using descriptive
statistics. Sensitivity analyses were performed separately to assess
the robustness of gravity-dependent conclusions to predefined
uncertainties in fiber orientation, material parameters, basal
constraints, and cavity-pressure assumptions.


